# mcp-server.dl — the MCP stdio server the +dl eval cells point at.
#
# The agent-eval harness gives a +dl cell exactly the built-in dl.what /
# dl.verb / dl.rows tools (src/mcp.rs's `builtin_tool_descriptors` /
# `builtin_tool_call`) and NOTHING else: no program-authored tool, no
# dl-specific hint text in the shared prompt (anti-grift protocol #2,
# plans/2026-07-10-agent-eval-harness.md). Those three tools are engine
# built-ins that any `--mcp` program advertises regardless of its own rules
# (`tools_list_merged` appends them; `tools/call` on a `dl.*` name is
# answered before the program's own dispatch ever runs), so this file needs
# no rules of its own — just the one @in(rpc)/@out(rpc) port pair `--mcp`
# requires to bind the profile at all (see examples/mcp-echo.dl).
#
#   dl bench/agent-eval/mcp-server.dl --mcp --no-daemon --db <scratch db>
#
# Run with cwd = the task's mutated worktree root (root is always cwd, never
# a flag — src/cli/root.rs), so dl.what/dl.verb/dl.rows read that worktree's
# own graph.
#
# `initialize` needs no rule here: src/mcp.rs's `handle_msg` answers it
# generically whenever no program rule heads it (gap-fill, mirrors how
# tools/list merges built-ins rather than replaces), so a zero-rule
# "just serve the built-ins" deployment completes the handshake for free.
rel req(id: text, method: text, params: text) @in(rpc).
rel resp(id: text, result: text) @out(rpc).
